# --- EX1 2026-09-07: linux/llm-stack enters the scan set. These 47 rows are the
# territory the extent gates could never see; every one was read and given a
# verdict in the same wave that widened SCAN, so none is a bare baseline. 34 of
# them say DEBT and name their seam -- that is the honest state of a benchmark
# harness nobody had reviewed for shape. docs/refactoring-backlog.md EX1
linux/scripts/build-cross-chain.sh | 890 | 986 -> 890 on 2026-09-30: comment sweep. PRIOR: 985 -> 986 on 2026-09-24: _chain_validate_stages also checks HAILO_NESTED_CACHE and HAILO_PYHAILORT_IPO (one call into hailo-build-lib.sh), for the same reason as the line before it. PRIOR: 983 -> 985 on 2026-09-24: _chain_validate_stages checks RUNTIME_WHEELS_SOURCE (one call into lib-runtime-wheels.sh plus its one-line why), so a typo stops the chain at its start rather than at the runtime lane hours later. PRIOR: 926 -> 983 on 2026-09-22: GPU variant chains. The variant block (CROSS_VARIANT -> exported ENABLE_*, per-variant LOG_DIR/chain-status, gpu start stage, 180G runtime budget, TensorRT default), _chain_validate_variant (a caller of stage-defs.sh cross_variant_refusal, which every entry point shares), the variant log-dir redirect, and _chain_refuse_live_sibling (the strictly-serial lock, claimed atomically) belong HERE: they decide stage order and lifecycle, the subject F2 keeps in one place. The tag logic itself went to tag-naming.sh and the graph to stage-defs.sh. 926 on 2026-09-15: +2 comment lines. chain-status.json is gitignored now, so the _chain_status_emit header no longer calls the repo-root copy TRACKED and says instead why the path is pinned to the repo root (readers look for it there). Same subject, same code. 912 -> 924 on 2026-09-10: _chain_warn_emulated_platform. Once the runtime artifact-source pin follows CROSS_BUILD_PLATFORM, a forgotten knob stops failing the FROM and instead succeeds under emulation for hours — the warn is the only thing that makes that visible at t=0. 890 -> 912 on 2026-09-07: DISK3's two call sites plus _chain_evict_slugs and the two measure functions it takes -- the extraction is +21 lines here and -9 cc in the guard, which is the trade F1 asked for. F2 reviewed 2026-09-04, NOT a split target: it is the orchestrator, and its subject is exactly the thing that must be read in one place — stage order, lifecycle, the EXIT trap and the disk guard. Its extractable helpers already live in 01-core (cross-stage-build.sh, disk-guard.sh, stage-defs.sh, chain-verify.sh); what remains is the sequencing that calls them. Inside the build closure. HISTORY: baseline 2026-09-03; +1 on 2026-09-04 turning _chain_on_exit's trailing `&&` list into an `if` — an EXIT trap ending on a false && returns 1 and fails a chain that finished green; 2026-09-04: DISK1: the in-stage sampler reaches for the filtered buildkit reclaim; +7 on 2026-09-05 wiring that reclaim into the two gates that own an episode — _disk_guard_reclaim_begin before each eviction pass, and a _disk_guard_buildkit_fallback plus its free-space re-read once threshold pruning falls short
